Unit tests

C++: gtest
Add gtest/gtest.h and use the standard macros. No ROS context is needed for pure logic:
#include &lt;gtest/gtest.h&gt;
#include "my_pkg/math_utils.hpp"

TEST(WrapAngleSuite, WrapsPositive) {
    EXPECT_NEAR(wrap_pi(4.0), 4.0 - 2 * M_PI, 1e-9);
}

TEST(WrapAngleSuite, WrapsNegative) {
    EXPECT_NEAR(wrap_pi(-4.0), -4.0 + 2 * M_PI, 1e-9);
}

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    ::testing::InitGoogleTest(&argc, argv);
    return RUN_ALL_TESTS();
}
Register in CMakeLists.txt:
find_package(ament_cmake_gtest REQUIRED)
ament_add_gtest(test_math_utils test/test_math_utils.cpp)
target_link_libraries(test_math_utils ${PROJECT_NAME})


Python: pytest
pytest auto-discovers any function named test_*. For pure-logic tests no ROS context is needed:
from my_pkg.math_utils import wrap_pi
import math

def test_wraps_positive():
    assert abs(wrap_pi(4.0) - (4.0 - 2 * math.pi)) &lt; 1e-9

def test_wraps_negative():
    assert abs(wrap_pi(-4.0) - (-4.0 + 2 * math.pi)) &lt; 1e-9
Register:
find_package(ament_cmake_pytest REQUIRED)
ament_add_pytest_test(test_math_utils test/test_math_utils.py)

OffboardControlMode heartbeat
PX4 requires a continuous OffboardControlMode heartbeat at ~3 Hz or it exits offboard mode. The active fields must match the command type being sent:
px4_msgs::msg::OffboardControlMode msg{};
msg.position          = false;
msg.velocity          = false;
msg.acceleration      = false;
msg.body_rate         = false;

// mode 1 — attitude
msg.attitude          = true;
msg.thrust_and_torque = false;
msg.direct_actuator   = false;

// mode 2 — thrust + torque
// msg.thrust_and_torque = true; (attitude and direct_actuator = false)

// mode 3 — direct actuator
// msg.direct_actuator = true; (attitude and thrust_and_torque = false)

msg.timestamp = node-&gt;get_clock()-&gt;now().nanoseconds() / 1000;
offboard_control_mode_pub-&gt;publish(msg);
The heartbeat timer and the controller output timer are independent:
// 3 Hz heartbeat
offboard_timer = create_wall_timer(333ms, [=]() { publishOffboardControlModeMsg(); });

// 100 Hz control loop
controller_timer = create_wall_timer(10ms,  [=]() { updateControllerOutput(); });

Mode 1: Attitude setpoint
Publish VehicleAttitudeSetpoint with a desired quaternion and collective thrust. The PX4 attitude controller tracks the quaternion error.
px4_msgs::msg::VehicleAttitudeSetpoint msg{};
msg.timestamp = node-&gt;get_clock()-&gt;now().nanoseconds() / 1000;

Eigen::Quaterniond q_ned = rotateQuaternionFromToENU_NED(desired_q);
msg.q_d[0] = q_ned.w();
msg.q_d[1] = q_ned.x();
msg.q_d[2] = q_ned.y();
msg.q_d[3] = q_ned.z();

msg.thrust_body[0] = 0.0;
msg.thrust_body[1] = 0.0;
msg.thrust_body[2] = -collective_thrust;   // NED: downward = negative

attitude_setpoint_pub-&gt;publish(msg);

Mode 2: Thrust + torque setpoint
Publish separate VehicleThrustSetpoint and VehicleTorqueSetpoint messages. PX4 uses these directly — no attitude controller in the loop.
px4_msgs::msg::VehicleThrustSetpoint thrust_msg{};
px4_msgs::msg::VehicleTorqueSetpoint torque_msg{};

uint64_t ts = node-&gt;get_clock()-&gt;now().nanoseconds() / 1000;
thrust_msg.timestamp = thrust_msg.timestamp_sample = ts;
torque_msg.timestamp = torque_msg.timestamp_sample = ts;

thrust_msg.xyz[0] = 0.0;
thrust_msg.xyz[1] = 0.0;
thrust_msg.xyz[2] = -collective_thrust;   // NED sign

// Rotate torque from controller FLU frame to FRD
Eigen::Vector3d tau_frd = rotateVectorFromToFRD_FLU(torque_flu);
torque_msg.xyz[0] = tau_frd.x();
torque_msg.xyz[1] = tau_frd.y();
torque_msg.xyz[2] = tau_frd.z();

thrust_pub-&gt;publish(thrust_msg);
torque_pub-&gt;publish(torque_msg);

Mode 3: Direct actuator commands
Publish ActuatorMotors with per-motor throttles in [0, 1]. Unused slots must be NaN.
px4_msgs::msg::ActuatorMotors msg{};
msg.control = {
    (float) throttles[0], (float) throttles[1],
    (float) throttles[2], (float) throttles[3],
    std::nanf("1"), std::nanf("1"), std::nanf("1"), std::nanf("1"),
    std::nanf("1"), std::nanf("1"), std::nanf("1"), std::nanf("1")
};
msg.reversible_flags = 0;
msg.timestamp = msg.timestamp_sample = node-&gt;get_clock()-&gt;now().nanoseconds() / 1000;
actuator_motors_pub-&gt;publish(msg);






Warning



Lockstep must be disabled for direct actuator control in Gazebo SITL. Set lockstep: false in the model’s model.sdf and in PX4’s SITL config.

C++ implementation
/* motor_allocator.hpp */
struct MotorAllocatorConfig {
    double arm_length = 0.25;       // m
    double k_T        = 8.54858e-6; // N/(rad/s)^2
    double k_M_ratio  = 0.019;      // k_m = k_M_ratio * k_T
    double omega_max  = 1047.2;     // rad/s
    double omega_min  = 150.0;      // rad/s
};

class MotorAllocator {
public:
    void initialize(const MotorAllocatorConfig& cfg) {
        cfg_ = cfg;
        build_inverse();
    }

    std::array&lt;float, 12&gt; allocate(double T, const Eigen::Vector3d& tau) const {
        Eigen::Vector4d wrench(tau(0), tau(1), tau(2), T);
        Eigen::Vector4d omega_sq = inv_ * wrench;

        std::array&lt;float, 12&gt; result;
        double range = cfg_.omega_max - cfg_.omega_min;
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 4; ++i) {
            double w = std::sqrt(std::max(0.0, omega_sq(i)));
            result[i] = static_cast&lt;float&gt;(std::clamp(
                (w - cfg_.omega_min) / range, 0.0, 1.0));
        }
        for (int i = 4; i &lt; 12; ++i)
            result[i] = std::nanf("1");   // unused slots must be NaN
        return result;
    }

private:
    void build_inverse() {
        const double kS = std::sin(M_PI / 4.0);
        double kt = cfg_.k_T,  km = cfg_.k_M_ratio * kt,  L = cfg_.arm_length;

        Eigen::Matrix4d B;
        B &lt;&lt; -kS,  kS,  kS, -kS,
               kS, -kS,  kS, -kS,
                1,   1,  -1,  -1,
                1,   1,   1,   1;

        Eigen::Vector4d k(kt * L, kt * L, km, kt);
        Eigen::Matrix4d A = k.asDiagonal() * B;
        inv_ = A.completeOrthogonalDecomposition().pseudoInverse();
    }

    MotorAllocatorConfig cfg_;
    Eigen::Matrix4d inv_;
};
The matrix is built once at initialization; allocate is called at 100 Hz in the control loop.

Publishing to PX4
auto msg = std::make_unique&lt;px4_msgs::msg::ActuatorMotors&gt;();
msg-&gt;timestamp        = px4_timestamp_us;
msg-&gt;timestamp_sample = px4_timestamp_us;
msg-&gt;reversible_flags = 0;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 12; ++i)
    msg-&gt;control[i] = motors[i];
motor_pub_-&gt;publish(std::move(msg));






Warning



PX4 requires a continuous ActuatorMotors stream from the moment offboard mode is entered. If the stream stops, PX4 exits offboard mode. Publish zeros while waiting for all sensor inputs to be ready — do not skip the publish call.

VehicleCommand helper
/* vehicle_command.hpp */
#pragma once
#include &lt;rclcpp/rclcpp.hpp&gt;
#include &lt;px4_msgs/msg/vehicle_command.hpp&gt;

inline void send_vehicle_command(
    rclcpp::Publisher&lt;px4_msgs::msg::VehicleCommand&gt;::SharedPtr& pub,
    rclcpp::Node* node,
    uint32_t command,
    float param1 = 0.0f,
    float param2 = 0.0f)
{
    px4_msgs::msg::VehicleCommand msg{};
    msg.timestamp       = node-&gt;get_clock()-&gt;now().nanoseconds() / 1000;
    msg.command         = command;
    msg.param1          = param1;
    msg.param2          = param2;
    msg.target_system   = 1;
    msg.target_component = 1;
    msg.source_system   = 1;
    msg.source_component = 1;
    msg.from_external   = true;
    pub-&gt;publish(msg);
}

Switching to offboard mode
#include &lt;px4_msgs/msg/vehicle_command.hpp&gt;

/* PX4_CUSTOM_MAIN_MODE_OFFBOARD = 6 */
send_vehicle_command(
    vehicle_cmd_pub_, this,
    px4_msgs::msg::VehicleCommand::VEHICLE_CMD_DO_SET_MODE,
    1.0f,   // base mode: custom
    6.0f);  // custom main mode: offboard
PX4 switches only if it has received OffboardControlMode messages recently. If the switch fails, VehicleStatus.nav_state stays at its prior value.

Full arming state machine
A typical controller node follows this pattern on startup:
/* Called at ~10 Hz in the main control timer */
void control_loop()
{
    /* 1. Always publish heartbeat first */
    publish_offboard_control_mode();

    /* 2. Always publish a setpoint (zeros are fine pre-arm) */
    publish_setpoint();

    /* 3. After a short warm-up, switch mode and arm */
    if (!mode_sent_ && ++tick_ &gt; 10) {   // ~1 s at 10 Hz
        switch_to_offboard();
        arm();
        mode_sent_ = true;
    }
}
The tick counter gives PX4 time to receive a few heartbeats before the mode switch is requested.

Takeoff after arming
After arming, the vehicle is on the ground with motors spinning at idle. Command a TrajectorySetpoint with a target altitude to begin the takeoff:
px4_msgs::msg::TrajectorySetpoint traj{};
traj.timestamp = get_clock()-&gt;now().nanoseconds() / 1000;

/* NED frame: negative z = above ground */
traj.position  = {0.0f, 0.0f, -2.0f};  // 2 m above ground
traj.yaw       = 0.0f;                  // North-facing
traj_pub_-&gt;publish(traj);
Use OffboardControlMode with position = true while climbing:
mode_msg.position = true;
mode_msg.velocity = false;
mode_msg.attitude = false;
Switch OffboardControlMode fields to match whichever control mode you activate once at altitude. See Low-Level Offboard Control.

Safety: continuous stream requirement
PX4 exits offboard mode if the OffboardControlMode stream stops for more than ~500 ms. It also disarms if the vehicle is commanded with zero throttle while on the ground in offboard mode for more than ~1 s.
Keep two independent timers:
// Heartbeat: 10 Hz is safe margin above the 2 Hz minimum
offboard_timer_ = create_wall_timer(100ms, [this]() {
    publish_offboard_control_mode();
});

// Control output: 100 Hz
control_timer_ = create_wall_timer(10ms, [this]() {
    publish_setpoint();
});
The timers are independent so a slow control computation does not starve the heartbeat.

